For example, it suffices to restrict oneself to finitely generated extensions of the prime field $Q$ -- one finds a conservative functor from the category of these fields to that of groupoids \inserted{(or of toposes)}, in the sense : a morphism of fields which gives an equivalence of groupoids (or of Toposes) is an \uline{isomorphism}.

\margin{in characteristic $\unsure{p>0|p\ne0}$ the functor is conservative if in addition one passes to the \unsure{quotient$^t$|quotient} category of that of fields where a finite radicial extension is considered trivial... (the functor $K\unsure{-|\longrightarrow}B_K$ or $K\unsure{-|\longrightarrow}\Pi_K$ factors through this quotient category.)}

When one takes arbitrary fields, the 2-functor $K\unsure{-|\longrightarrow}B_K$ or $K\longrightarrow\Pi_K$ \inserted{or $(K,\bar K)\unsure{-|\longrightarrow}E_{\bar K/K}$} is however far from being faithful. Thus, if $K$ is separably closed, $B_K$ is the ``point topos'', $\Pi_K$ the point groupoid, $E_{\bar K/K}\simeq(1)$ -- it is clear that the morphisms between separably closed fields are not described by the morphisms between their étale toposes, or fundamental groupoids ! For this reason, it is appropriate to associate to a field $K$ an object finer than $B_K$ or $\Pi_K$,
